      "json": {
        "id": "630018",
        "etag": "0d5f5696-0533-4c4f-a2f7-86c5319b2d2c",
        "createdOn": "2024-11-08T01:35:29.349Z",
        "createdBy": "3518021",
        "modifiedOn": "2025-04-09T05:07:55.738Z",
        "modifiedBy": "3518021",
        "title": "",
        "markdown": "**Dataset Description**\n\nThe Eye-BCI multimodal dataset provides an extensive collection capturing simultaneous electroencephalography (EEG), electrooculogram (EOG), electromyogram (EMG), high-speed video recordings, and synchronized eye-tracking. Participants' mental states and selected physical characteristics are also included, creating a solid foundation for examining ocular artifacts and eye-movement features in Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) applications.\n\nFour experimental paradigms are recorded to elicit various sensory-motor responses and their influence on ocular activity: Motor Imagery (MI), Motor Execution (ME), Steady-State Visually Evoked Potentials (SSVEP), and P300 speller tasks. Within the P300 paradigm, trials are organized into two folders to differentiate word-length conditions, one for four-letter target words and the other for five-letter targets.\n\n**Dataset information**\n\nA detailed description of the dataset is available in the following paper:\nGuttmann-Flury, E., Sheng, X. & Zhu, X. Dataset combining EEG, eye-tracking, and high-speed video for ocular activity analysis across BCI paradigms. Sci Data 12, 587 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-04861-9\n\n**Participants**\nA total of 31 volunteers, including both left- and right-handed individuals, participated in this experiment across 63 sessions, amounting to approximately 46 hours of recording. The dataset includes 2520 trials for each of the Motor Imagery (MI), Motor Execution (ME), and Steady-State Visually Evoked Potentials (SSVEP) paradigms, and 5670 trials for the P300 paradigm. During the initial session, participants completed a questionnaire assessing their [demographic information, physiological details, and psychophysiological states](https://www.synapse.org/Synapse:syn64087108). Additionally, an a priori sample size calculation was performed to ensure adequate power for the analysis of signals related to blinks as well as cortical sources activated during the different paradigms[^1^](https://doi.org/10.1109/embc.2019.8857482).\n\n**Experimental Setup**\nAll code for replicating this experiment, including instructions for simultaneous EEG, EMG, EOG, and high-speed video recordings along with eye-tracker synchronization, is available at [https://github.com/QinXinlan/EEG-experiment-to-understand-differences-in-blinking/](https://github.com/QinXinlan/EEG-experiment-to-understand-differences-in-blinking/).\n\n**How to access the data?**\n\nAll data can be accessed directly in the data folder. \n\n**Data Structure**\n\nData files from the four software programs follow a consistent naming format: **NameParadigm-S-XX-Sess-Y**, where XX represents the participant ID ranging from 01 to 31, and Y indicates the session number, ranging from 1 to 3. Questionnaires are summarized for each session using the format: **S-XX-Sess-Y**. Sessions for each participant are organized by folder, with subfolders for each recording modality.\n\nFor streamlined analysis, supplementary columns have been added to the EEG files, incorporating key information from E-Prime (e.g., Trig and Cues columns) and the high-speed Phantom camera (PhanFrame, PhanTime, RelTime, RecordingTimestamp, and LocalTimeStamp). Additionally, a \"Blinks\" column indicates blink peaks, coded as 0 (no blink) or 1 (blink peak). \n\nThe EEG recordings were conducted using 62 electrodes placed according to the extended 10/20 system, ensuring comprehensive spatial coverage of brain activity. However, we recommend excluding the following electrodes from analysis, as they were identified as either malfunctioning or exhibiting bridging effects: PO3, F1, POZ, OZ, F3, O2, P8, PO7, FC3, P7, and P4[^2^]().\n\nThe \"Trig\" column represents trial numbers, ranging from 1 to 50 in the P3005L paradigm and from 1 to 40 in other paradigms. The \"Cues\" column provides the sequence within each trial, specific to each paradigm:\n\n${image?fileName=Chronology%2Epng&align=None&scale=100&responsive=true&altText=}\n\nFor the Tobii eye tracker, data include gaze coordinates (GazePointLeftX/Y, GazePointRightX/Y, GazePointX/Y), pupil positions (CamLeftX/Y, CamRightX/Y), and distances from the eye tracker to each eye (DistanceLeft, DistanceRight). Pupil size (PupilLeft, PupilRight) and validity codes (ValidityLeft, ValidityRight) are also recorded, indicating the system\u2019s confidence in tracking accuracy.\n\n**Subject Information for Multimodal Data**\n${image?fileName=Table 1%2Epng&align=None&scale=100&responsive=true&altText=Table 1%2E Demographic description and a few physical characteristics of the participants}\n\n**Dataset license**\n[Eye-BCI multimodal dataset](https://doi.org/10.7303/syn64005218) \u00a9 2024 by Eva Guttmann-Flury, Xinjun Sheng, and Xiangyang Zhu is licensed under the [CC0 License](https://creativecommons.org/public-domain/cc0/)",
        "attachmentFileHandleIds": [
          "149567005",
          "149839473"
        ]
      }
